    "readme": "# Legitimate Inline Scripts\n\nMoving all your own JavaScript into **same-origin external files** is usually the simplest way to keep a strict CSP. When a trusted inline script really has to stay inline, authorize just that _one_ script with a per-response [nonce](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Global_attributes/nonce).\n\nGenerate a fresh, unpredictable value for every HTML response:\n\n```go\nnonceBytes := make([]byte, 16)\nif _, err := rand.Read(nonceBytes); err != nil {\n    return err\n}\nnonce := base64.StdEncoding.EncodeToString(nonceBytes)\n```\n\nInclude the value in `script-src`:\n\n```http\nContent-Security-Policy: script-src 'self' 'nonce-z7G4mN2qL8vP1sR5'\n```\n\nThen place the matching value on the trusted script element:\n\n```html\n<script nonce=\"z7G4mN2qL8vP1sR5\">\n  window.location.assign(\"/orders/42\");\n</script>\n```\n\nThe browser only executes inline scripts that carry the nonce from that response's policy. An attacker who can inject markup can't predict the next nonce, and an old nonce doesn't authorize a script in a new response.\n\n> [!warning]\n>\n> Never reuse a fixed nonce or accept one provided from the client's request.\n\n## Assignment\n\nBearly Secure already generates a fresh CSP nonce for every response and renders it on PawPal's trusted redirect script, but its policy ignores the nonce. **Allow only that nonce in `script-src`.**\n\n1. [ ] In `internal/httpserver/middleware.go`, update `contentSecurityPolicy` to load the response nonce with `httpx.CSPNonce(request.Context())` and add the matching `'nonce-<value>'` source to `script-src` without allowing `'unsafe-inline'`.\n2. [ ] Start the app, log in as Mabel, and confirm PawPal redirects automatically after checkout.\n\n_With Bearly Secure still running_, **run and submit** the CLI tests from the project root.\n"
